            <ArticleTitle>[Non-cardiac thoracic pain. Diagnosis, differential diagnosis and therapy].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>113-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In about 50% of the cases, non-cardiac chest pain is due to hypomotile or hypermotile functional disorders of the esophagus. X-ray examination, endoscopy and manometry, possibly with provocation with edrophonium, confirm the diagnosis. Gastro-esophageal reflux is found in 40%, motility disorders in 20%, and an irritable esophagus in 40% of the cases. For diagnosis ex juvantibus, gastroprokinetic drugs or H2-blockers, nitro compounds and calcium antagonists may be useful.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
